-- #1648 — an `impl … requires` method whose BODY NEVER USES ITS DICT is EMITTED at
-- arity 1 and CALLED with 2. `check`, `run` and `medaka build` all accept this program
-- at exit 0; the BUILT BINARY dies with a segmentation fault before printing anything.
--
-- ⭐ HAND-DERIVED EXPECTED VALUE (not captured, not inherited — this suite's own header
-- says a captured golden would enshrine whatever the engine does, and the engine is the
-- thing under suspicion here):
--
--   1. `bar : a -> Int` is declared at the interface, so at the impl head `Box a` it is
--      `bar : Box a -> Int`.
--   2. `Box 7` is the only constructor of `Box`, whose parameter `a` is PHANTOM (the
--      constructor stores an `Int` and nothing of type `a`). So `Box 7 : Box a` with `a`
--      still free, and the impl's `requires Eq a` obligation is discharged against it.
--   3. The impl's single method body matches `Box n => n`, and `Box`'s only field here
--      holds the literal `7`.
--   4. ⇒ `bar (Box 7)` is 7, and A CORRECT COMPILER'S BINARY PRINTS `7` AND EXITS 0.
--
-- What actually happens (measured first-hand, see claim.txt): the binary exits 139 having
-- printed NOTHING. The emitted IR for this file, read directly with `build --keep-ir`:
--
--   define i64 @mdk_impl_Box_bar(i64 %arg0) { … }        -- arity 1
--   %t4 = call i64 @mdk_impl_Box_bar(i64 0, i64 %t3)     -- 2 arguments, leading dict NULL
--
-- so the definition's one surviving parameter binds POSITIONALLY to the dict slot.
--
-- ⚠️ THE DICT IS UNUSED BY CONSTRUCTION AND CANNOT BE MADE LIVE IN *THIS* FILE — that is
-- the point of the shape, not an oversight. `a` is phantom, so the body has no value of
-- type `a` to compare and no way to force `Eq a`. control.mdk is the variant that gives
-- `Box` an `a`-typed field precisely so its body CAN use the dict; see claim.txt for why
-- that costs the control one extra flipped dimension.
data Box a = Box Int

interface Same a where
  bar : a -> Int

impl Same (Box a) requires Eq a where
  bar b = match b
    Box n => n

main = println (bar (Box 7))
